###########################
#  Initialization

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
  "PGstandard.pl",
  "MathObjects.pl",
  "parserRadioButtons.pl",
  "PGML.pl",
  "PGcourse.pl",
);

TEXT(beginproblem());


###########################
#  Setup

Context("Numeric");
##J1 Q2.1
$radio1 = RadioButtons(
  ["Curve","Piecewise curve","Both", "Neither"],
  "Piecewise curve", # correct answer
  last => ["Curve","Piecewise curve","Both", "Neither"], # can be a list
);

##J1 Q2.2
$radio2 = RadioButtons(
  ["Curve","Piecewise curve","Both", "Neither"],
  "Both", # correct answer
  last => ["Curve","Piecewise curve","Both", "Neither"], # can be a list 
);

##J1 Q2.3
$radio3 = RadioButtons(
  ["Curve","Piecewise curve","Both", "Neither"],
  "Piecewise curve", # correct answer
  last => ["Curve","Piecewise curve","Both", "Neither"], # can be a list
);

##J1 Q2.4
$radio4 = RadioButtons(
  ["Curve","Piecewise curve","Both", "Neither"],
  "Both", # correct answer
  last => ["Curve","Piecewise curve","Both", "Neither"], # can be a list
);



###########################
#  Main text

BEGIN_TEXT
Recall two key definitions. $BR

	A set \( C \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n \) is a \( \textbf{curve} \) if there exists a simple regular parametrization of \( C \). $BR
	A set \( C \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n \) is a \( \textbf{piecewise curve} \) if \( C \) can be written as a finite union of curves \( C_1,\dots,C_k \) such that \( C_i \cap C_j \) is finite for any pair of distinct curves \( C_i \) and \( C_j. \) $BR

For each set \( C \subseteq \mathbb{R}^2 \), determine if \( C \) is a curve, a piecewise curve, both, or neither. 
$BR $BR
\{ image( "j1-q2-graphs.png", width=>781, height=>466) \} $BR $BR


2.1 
END_TEXT

BEGIN_PGML
[@ $radio1->buttons() @]*
END_PGML

BEGIN_TEXT
$BR
2.2 
END_TEXT

